I mean, Photon was still like a kind of MVP, the tech was good, but not as good as it is now.

30
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:54,880
So it wasn't like I absolutely needed it yet.

31
00:02:55,120 --> 00:03:06,800
I think when we started to get traction on, I think it was, yeah, eBay early days as marketplaces when, then people, we had influencers talking about us and like people in the comments., the comments were, I need it.

32
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:07,600
I'm on Android.

33
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:08,320
I'm on Android.

34
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:09,120
I'm on Android.

35
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:18,160
So I think when you start doing marketing, you feel like you're paying for actually you're paying for some influencers and then all the comments are, When is Android coming?

36
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:18,880
and all that stuff.

37
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:20,160
So I remember that now.

38
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:24,080
So you feel people are asking when you start to eat product market fit.

39
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:31,920
I think before that, you can tell yourself like, Oh, it would be better because then the variety loop, like the famous K factor, all that stuff would be better.

40
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:33,280
But I'm not sure.

41
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:34,880
It's like you don't feel the pressure.

42
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:44,320
If you had to do it again, would you have launched simultaneously Android and iOS or do you feel like it was better to go ahead and kind prove the product market fit on iOS first.

43
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:45,280
Yeah, never, never.

44
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:50,640
Like, I would be exactly the same iOS and then one or two years later, Android.

45
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:57,840
And I do think early adopters of apps as a general rule of thinking would be more on iOS than Android.

46
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:03,840
And as a general rule, but even more so for the photo space, photo video category.

47
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:09,040
When you started to build up to the launch on Android, did you build up a whole separate Android team?

48
00:04:09,280 --> 00:04:11,200
And is that how you run Android to this day?

49
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:16,160
Is like you spin up a whole separate team that works in conjunction with the iOS team?

50
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:18,160
Yeah, we have two different teams.

51
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:25,920
So with time, what we developed is a shared engine that is running like the pixel rendering part.

52
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:29,840
So everything that is pixel rendering and AI is common.

53
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:41,680
We do have some native like device algorithm that run on Apple that is very, I mean, we didn't even try on Android, like it's too fragmented the hardware to run on device machine learning inference.

54
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,880
But the engine is all the same on iOS Android now.

55
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:56,640
So how does that work out today as far as you're still doing earlier explorations on one platform and like you would maybe test something first on iOS and then move it to Android?

56
00:04:56,640 --> 00:05:07,600
And then move it to Android or now do you kind of more simultaneously release on both platforms or are you even like experimenting with certain things that you think might work better on Android on Android and then bring them to iOS?

57
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:09,840
How do you manage that development process?

58
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:15,840
We don't have like a tightly loose package where we want to release everything at the same time for all platforms.

59
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:18,560
Now, I mean, we have iOS, Android and even web now.

60
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:23,360
The way we think about it is we need to run like a I mean, so like you have two legs.

61
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:26,320
Like if you really tie them together, you don't make like big steps.

62
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:30,720
We try to have some flexibility to make sure we move fast because that's the key to success for a startup.

63
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:34,800
And we kind of use like resources when it's easier.

64
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:41,360
I do think there are a few things where we try where it makes more sense based on the platform to try on different versions.

65
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:45,280
So I don't know, there are more experiments or it's easier to track.

66
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:46,480
I mean, I'll give you an example.

67
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:49,440
You can create a team in Photorum and you can invite someone.

68
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:55,840
For Apple, it's you can't really track because of privacy reasons through the app store if you're inviting someone.

69
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,280
So it's much easier to have a good benchmark of what would work, what would be the dream path on Android and iOS.

70
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:04,640
So I mean, we're not like we depend on resources.

71
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:05,360
How big is the team?

72
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:07,040
IOS team is bigger than Android.

73
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:13,040
So it's usually it's iOS first, but there are some stuff where it it makes more sense to try Android, but we would go with Android first.

74
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:17,200
And then how do you think about monetization between the two platforms?

75
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:26,400
I know this is something where Photo Room is maybe a little bit unique from other apps, but how do you think broadly about monetization amongst the two platforms?

76
00:06:26,560 --> 00:06:28,560
It's not exactly the same, the two platforms.

77
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:37,680
What I remember quite clearly is, so I was at GoPro before, and it was very challenging to have a good monetization on the Android side.

78
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:44,640
And what I learned is that they're, well, first, difficult to build a good video app on Android.

79
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:50,000
The IPIs from Apple are really good on the video side and the hardware is less fragmented.

80
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:57,280
Like, it's not like there's still some devices where you think there are some GPUs and they're not here or like it's not plugged in the right way.

81
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:00,880
Apple owns hardware and software, obviously, it's a bit easier.

82
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:05,040
And then there's like, I think Apple has a very like a amazing brand on the photo side.

83
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:09,520
So if you're a photo hobbyist, like the reality is you're probably going to buy an iPhone.

84
00:07:09,680 --> 00:07:12,320
Like, you're really passionate about the, about photography.

85
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:22,800
And so what I noticed at GoPro is basically we were doing like people were doing three X more revenue on average on apps and there were like two X more people or maybe four X more revenue and two X more people.

86
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:31,520
So basically it was one to ten., the ratio of like a user, the RPU by kind of average when I was looking at some benchmarks, iOS to Android.

87
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:33,440
And then I dig into the photo space.

88
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:36,080
Basically, the ratio for photo apps was thirty x.

89
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:40,160
So the value of a user on iOS was thirty x bigger than on Android.

90
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:41,760
So that was a big learning.

91
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:44,960
There's a lot of marketing from Apple about photography.

92
00:07:45,120 --> 00:07:53,760
You actually, if you look at the keynote, it's almost as if, well, we should call that a camera more than a phone now because like that's the marketing cell.

93
00:07:54,080 --> 00:07:57,760
That's why a lot of the reasons why people upgrade is because the camera got better.

94
00:07:58,080 --> 00:08:00,880
So if you're like taking photos of your family, it matters.

95
00:08:01,360 --> 00:08:07,440
You want to spend time on photography, then you're probably buying an iPhone, especially in the biggest market of all the US.

96
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:14,320
And that's like a huge, that makes a huge bias towards like people spending money on the photo apps and the app store compared to the Play store.

97
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:17,120
What happened with Photom is like we got very international.

98
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:23,040
And the most important part is that people buy Photom because Photom helps them make money.

99
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:24,960
Like we helped your store grow.

100
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:38,160
We helped you as an e commerce owner, as an entrepreneur, as a restaurant owner to have good quality imagery to like present yourself on marketplaces and social networks in your email.

101
00:08:39,040 --> 00:08:41,520
And so, I mean, as a business owner, it doesn't matter.

102
00:08:41,840 --> 00:08:44,560
Like, there's no filter on your IOS or Android person.

103
00:08:44,800 --> 00:08:46,320
There's, like, actually no bias.

104
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:49,680
And so that's why, at the end of the day, like, everyone is working.

105
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:51,360
A lot of people have small business owners.

106
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:53,360
There are a lot of them in the world who are on Android.

107
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:54,240
And so they spend more.

108
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:55,120
They don't care.

109
00:08:55,360 --> 00:08:58,960
They just spend the money that the subscription that makes them more successful.

110
00:08:59,120 --> 00:09:00,320
So there's no bias in that.

111
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:07,680
And so we're much more successful, like, relatively on the photospace on Android than the other apps that are, like, hugely biased towards IOS.

112
00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:11,440
And because of the API, also, you see so many apps that never went on Android.

113
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:15,840
So it's like, it never made sense economically for them where it's very dis different for us.

114
00:09:16,080 --> 00:09:21,360
You've mentioned several times the fragmentation of devices on Android and the challenges there.

115
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:28,880
Do you have any advice for folks who are building on Android, as far as like QA or like minimum required specs for the app?

116
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:34,480
How do you think about making sure that people do have a good experience on Android devices?

117
00:09:35,040 --> 00:09:40,160
The best way to make sure you have a good experience is it's a very practical advice.

118
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:47,920
But I remember the thing I did is like for a year or so, I just switched as a funder as we were building the app to Android.

119
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:53,600
And so I was like only using that to make sure it's important like you pay attention to details.

120
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:05,040
So that was like a first very practical, just like remove your SIM card from your iPhone, buy a good Android device and make sure like you test like it's dog fooding is the key.

121
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:13,280
And then keep in mind also that like iOS people update and upgrade their device to latest iOS a bit more than on Android.

122
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:14,720
And Apple is really good at that.

123
00:10:14,960 --> 00:10:20,000
They always give like they always give this like last feature that is like fun that makes people move.

124
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:25,520
And I think it's, I don't know, behind the scenes, but I think they really care about that and they advertise that a lot.

125
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:31,840
So yeah, keep in mind you can't like maybe on iOS you can keep two versions of the major OS and Android, you need to keep a bit more.

126
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:37,760
Do you have a separate QA team or what does quality assurance look like on Android?

127
00:10:38,560 --> 00:10:48,720
And I mean, do you even have like a does the Android team have twenty or thirty devices that they test on or how do you think about that quality assurance side of things for Android?

128
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:56,400
We don't have QA at Foturm, so that's the one thing, like everyone is in charge of their own bugs and everyone is using the app.

129
00:10:56,880 --> 00:11:09,200
We do have a strong beta S fly Android Intel, so these are managed in different ways, but where people like test the app for a week and they have the latest feature, but sometimes you it's AUGY and we look at the analytics what's happening.

130
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:11,760
If there is anything weird, we have some signals.

131
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:17,600
I mean, Photorum is lucky enough to have like a product that is you can use in your everyday life.

132
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:21,120
So we have people using it regularly and the team internally using the app.

133
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:32,880
I you say like I want to be fair with the Android team, like one of the difficult things is the distribution of iOS versus Android in most companies is heavily biased towards iOS.

134
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:36,960
So it's more, it's much more difficult to get people testing an Android.

135
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:47,840
What we do give is like anyone that wants an Android as a Photorum employee who is on iOS and wants Android and is going to use it or test it or try it or is a PM, which is you can have two devices if you're going to help the Android team.

136
00:11:48,560 --> 00:11:54,880
Any other learnings that you've had now operating on Android for almost five years that you think folks could take away?

137
00:11:55,440 --> 00:12:05,680
I mean, one of the challenges that we haven't really solved is how do you solve for design and, you know, product because most people and most PMs would go on iOS at our scale.

138
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:11,200
I'm sure like if you're ten X bigger, you can have someone that is specific, you can have a design that is specific to Android.

139
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:14,080
But reality is like, it's like two different languages, you know?

140
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:17,280
It's the two like you, I mean, you can take Portuguese and Spanish.

141
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:19,840
Like, it's some and grammar is similar, but some.

142
00:12:20,400 --> 00:12:25,680
like ideomagic like expressions are different and then you can make kind of a faux pas or stuff like this.

143
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:32,320
So give some freedom and make sure that the Android team like understands and that you say no, you understand the differences.

144
00:12:32,560 --> 00:12:34,800
Like, I mean, the obvious example is the share icon.

145
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:46,880
I had so much debate with the design team and like a product about like the designs and you see this little arrow compared to the three circles like the branching on Android that are that is the share icon.

146
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:50,880
Like people on Android, they don't understand the iOS icon and it doesn't look native.

147
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:53,600
It's like you're using your word in another language in their platform.

148
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:57,040
So if you're making the bet and that's from bet that's like native.

149
00:12:57,600 --> 00:13:01,200
is better, then yeah, you need to adapt and adapt the language.

150
00:13:01,280 --> 00:13:04,880
And we don't have two versions of the designs, that's one for iOS, one for Android.

151
00:13:05,120 --> 00:13:06,960
So it makes things a bit difficult.

152
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:11,440
But yeah, you have to allow for this freedom based on the native language.

153
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:17,040
Any last tips that you think people should be thinking about the differences between iOS and Android?

154
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:22,800
There's one big thing that people tend to forget is Apple is 30% commission on a new subscription.

155
00:13:22,880 --> 00:13:26,160
They have like some people, if you renew, stay on 15%.

156
00:13:26,400 --> 00:13:28,320
Android's all 15%.

157
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:36,800
So it does make a difference for your margin, especially for a company on margin like Photonroom where you have GPU costs for machine learning and GNI.

158
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:38,800
So it does make a difference.

159
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:39,200
Yeah.

160
00:13:39,520 --> 00:13:39,600
Yeah.

161
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:41,040
I think a lot of people forget that.

162
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:44,160
I forget that often is thirty percent versus fifteen percent.

163
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:59,920
You know, and for those of you maybe aren't familiar with this on Android, Google made the decision three or four years ago that just all subscriptions, it doesn't apply to games and consumables and single in-app purchases, but on all subscriptions it's fifteen percent across the board.

164
00:14:00,240 --> 00:14:10,160
And as Matt was alluding to on iOS, once a user has retained for more than a year, you do get bumped down to fifteen percent, but on initial conversion.

165
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:13,360
That spread instantly gives you more margin.

166
00:14:13,520 --> 00:14:16,480
So definitely something from a business perspective to think about.

167
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:22,400
Even if your average revenue per user is lower on Android, your margin is actually higher on those pairs.

168
00:14:22,480 --> 00:14:24,480
So yeah, great tip for folks to keep in mind.

169
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:26,720
Yeah, but build on Android is great too.

170
00:14:26,880 --> 00:14:31,920
Okay, anything else you wanted to share as we're wrapping up, any especially great roles you're hiring for these days?

171
00:14:32,400 --> 00:14:32,880
Yeah, yeah.

172
00:14:33,040 --> 00:14:34,160
I mean, we keep growing.

173
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:42,560
And so we're hiring both in engineering, both platform IOS and Android, and also like we're hiring for the top of funnel of the app store.

174
00:14:42,720 --> 00:14:56,320
Like if you're an app store hacker, you know all the details of that and you want to look like very top of funnel Nolof apps and actually play with there are many things like act new things, use Photroom API to make screenshots, you know, all these things, like it's a very interesting role.

175
00:14:56,480 --> 00:14:56,960
Awesome.

